(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-diagnostic-tests-quiz"]={"title":"Diagnostic Tests Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What separates a diagnostic test from a screening test?","choices":["Diagnostic tests are for symptomatic individuals","Diagnostic tests are for asymptomatic individuals","Diagnostic tests are always more sensitive","Screening tests are always the gold standard"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and screening tests are for asymptomatic ones, which is why the same test can be either depending on whom it is applied to. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the practical use of a receiver operating characteristic curve?","choices":["Determining the prevalence of the disease","Converting pre-test odds to post-test odds","Deciding whether to test in parallel or serially","Comparing alternative tests for the same diagnosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since it displays the whole trade-off between true and false positives rather than a single pair of values. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"As a rule of thumb, what does a likelihood ratio of 0.1 do?","choices":["Lowers it by about 30 percentage points","Lowers the probability by about 45 percentage points","Lowers it by about 15 percentage points","Lowers it to one tenth of its value"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, mirroring the 45 point rise given by a ratio of 10, which is why below 0.1 counts as strong evidence to rule the disease out. — Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios"},{"q":"How should a screening campaign combine tests?","choices":["Screen with a highly specific test, confirm with a sensitive one","Use the gold standard for screening","Screen with a highly sensitive test, confirm with a highly specific one","Use two tests of equal sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the first pass should miss as few cases as possible and the second should avoid committing healthy people to further investigation. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which test should generally be performed first in a serial strategy?","choices":["The one with the highest specificity","The one with the highest sensitivity","The gold standard test","The one the patient prefers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because it avoids unnecessary testing of people without the disease, unless another test is cheaper or less risky. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What should be considered when two tests disagree?","choices":["Which test was more expensive","Which test was performed first","Whether they ran in parallel or serially","Which test the patient prefers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the two strategies do different things to the combined sensitivity and specificity, which changes how the disagreement should be read. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is the rule-of-thumb probability shift applied?","choices":["It is added to the pre-test probability","It replaces the pre-test probability","It is multiplied by the pre-test probability","It is subtracted from the specificity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why the same likelihood ratio produces a very different answer depending on how likely the disease was before the test. — Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios"},{"q":"As a rule of thumb, what does a likelihood ratio of 10 do to the probability of disease?","choices":["Raises it by about 30 percentage points","Raises it by about 45 percentage points","Raises it by about 15 percentage points","Multiplies it by ten"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the shift is ADDED to the pre-test probability rather than replacing it, which is why the starting point still matters. — Assigned reading: Likelihood Ratios"},{"q":"What is a gold standard test?","choices":["The cheapest test that detects the disease","A procedure believed to identify the disease with certainty","The test with the highest positive predictive value","The first test ordered in any work-up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with culture for streptococcal throat infection, biopsy for cancer, cardiac catheterisation for coronary disease and endoscopy for peptic ulcer as the examples. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"With a sensitivity of 99 per cent and specificity of 95 per cent, positive predictive value rises from 17 to 51 per cent as prevalence rises from one to five per cent. What does that demonstrate?","choices":["The test became more accurate","Specificity rises with prevalence","Negative predictive value is unaffected by prevalence","Predictive value depends heavily on prevalence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the test itself did not change: the same sensitivity and specificity produced a threefold difference in what a positive result means. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How is prevalence expressed in the lettered cells?","choices":["a plus b, over all four cells","a over a plus b","a plus c, over all four cells","d over b plus d"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, being everyone with the disease over everyone tested. It is also the pre-test probability in the population of interest. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"A rapid antigen test has a sensitivity of 65 per cent and specificity of 96 per cent. What is its positive likelihood ratio?","choices":["About 0.36","About 16","About 1.5","About 0.65"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: 0.65 divided by 0.04 gives 16.25, so a positive result makes the infection about sixteen times more likely. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why are predictive values influenced by prevalence?","choices":["They include both diseased and healthy people","They are calculated within a single disease column","Prevalence changes the sensitivity of the test","Prevalence alters the gold standard result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because each reads across a test-result row, which mixes the two disease columns in a proportion set by how common the disease is. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio describe?","choices":["How much a result changes the probability of the condition","The proportion of tested patients who have the disease","The probability that a positive result is correct","The reliability of the test on repetition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its great advantage is that it is not affected by prevalence, unlike the predictive values. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which question should be asked about a study of a new diagnostic test?","choices":["Was the study published in a high-impact journal?","Did every subject undergo both tests?","How many authors contributed to the study?","Was the test developed commercially?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside whether the study included a spectrum of patients and whether those conducting each test were blinded to the other's result. That means the new test and the gold standard. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does diagnosing disease require?","choices":["A gold standard test in every patient","The highest available sensitivity in every case","Testing every patient who presents","A reliable method of detecting its presence or absence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the task is to identify who has the disease and who does not, and that is only as good as the method used to separate them. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the trade-off of serial compared with parallel testing?","choices":["More tests used, but less time taken","Fewer tests and less time","Fewer tests used, but more time taken","More tests and more time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why the choice comes down to how urgent the diagnosis is against how costly or risky the tests are. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does parallel testing generally increase?","choices":["Sensitivity and negative predictive value","Specificity and positive predictive value","The prevalence of the disease","The reliability of each individual test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so disease is less likely to be missed. How much sensitivity improves depends on how well each test catches the cases the other misses. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What kind of characteristics are sensitivity and specificity?","choices":["Fairly fixed characteristics of the test","Characteristics that vary with prevalence","Characteristics of the patient rather than the test","Characteristics that change with each result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which makes them useful when planning a screening campaign but less helpful when explaining to a patient what their own result means. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is negative predictive value calculated?","choices":["True negatives over true negatives plus false positives","True positives over true positives plus false positives","True positives over true positives plus false negatives","True negatives over true negatives plus false negatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, reading across the test-negative row, which is why it depends on how many diseased people were in the population tested. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is positive predictive value?","choices":["The probability that someone with disease tests positive","The probability that someone testing negative is disease free","The probability that someone without disease tests negative","The probability that someone testing positive has the disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, calculated as true positives over true positives plus false positives, which reads across the test-positive row. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is sensitivity?","choices":["The probability a person without disease tests negative","The probability a person testing positive has disease","The probability a person testing negative is disease free","The probability a person with disease tests positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and read as a probability it runs from zero, identifying none of the diseased, to one, identifying all of them. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which criterion in the modified Centor score can score minus one?","choices":["Absence of cough","Age over 45","Temperature above 38 degrees","Tonsillar swelling or exudate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: ages 3 to 14 score one point, 15 to 44 score nothing, and over 45 scores minus one, because the infection becomes less likely with age. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Written in the lettered cells, what is positive predictive value?","choices":["a over a plus c","d over c plus d","a over a plus b","d over b plus d"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, dividing true positives by everyone who tested positive, which is why the mix of diseased and healthy people in the population affects it. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"In that same study, 9,895 of 9,975 patients without ulcers tested negative. What is the specificity?","choices":["99 per cent","80 per cent","20 per cent","50 per cent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and note that the 80 false positives outnumber the 20 true positives, which is what will wreck the positive predictive value despite this high specificity. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A test has a positive predictive value of 85 per cent. What does that mean for a patient who tests positive?","choices":["The test detects 85 per cent of people with the disease","85 per cent of healthy people test negative","They have an 85 per cent chance of really having the disease","They have an 85 per cent chance of being disease free"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this is why predictive values are the useful numbers at the bedside: they answer the question the patient is actually asking. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which statement captures how common and uncommon disease present?","choices":["Rare diseases present typically more often than common ones present atypically","Uncommon presentations of common disease beat common presentations of rare disease","Common and rare diseases are equally likely at presentation","Atypical presentations always indicate a rare disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is the reasoning behind thinking of horses rather than zebras, tempered by the reminder that no disease is rare to the patient who has it. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What happens to false positives when a disease has low prevalence?","choices":["There are usually fewer of them","There are usually more of them","Their number is unaffected by prevalence","They become true positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the pool of healthy people is so much larger that even a small false positive rate produces many of them relative to the few true cases. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What limits the diagnosis of disease?","choices":["The prevalence of the disease alone","The number of tests ordered","How good the tests used to measure it are","The cost of the gold standard"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the rest follows from it: sensitivity and specificity guide test selection, while predictive values describe what a given result means. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"As prevalence falls, what happens to the two predictive values?","choices":["Both fall together","Both rise together","Positive predictive value rises and negative falls","Positive predictive value falls and negative rises"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because there are fewer true positive cases to be found, so a positive result is more often wrong and a negative result more often right. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"}]};
